# Per-site Cloudflare credentials for cf-rules.sh.
#
# NEVER put a filled-in cf.env inside vendor/augustash/claude-config — this package ships
# to every project, so a real token here would propagate to all of them. That is the only
# hard rule; the site repo itself is fine, and is the better home.
#
# PREFERRED — a gitignored dir in the site, next to the site it belongs to:
#   mkdir -p .cloudflare && cp <this file> .cloudflare/cf.env && chmod 600 .cloudflare/cf.env
#   echo '/.cloudflare/' >> .gitignore
#   git check-ignore -v .cloudflare/cf.env      # verify before writing the token
#
# A directory, not a bare file: cf-rules.sh writes <site-dir>/out/ (pulled rulesets and
# pre-write backups), and that must not be committed either. One ignore rule covers both.
#
# Keep it in the site because a parallel ~/.config tree is a place nobody looks — a
# credential filed there goes missing the moment you stop thinking about it.
#
# FALLBACK — ~/.config/cloudflare/<site>/cf.env, when the repo is not yours to add an
# ignore rule to, when the deploy artifact is built from the repo in a way that would
# sweep the file in regardless, or when git check-ignore does not confirm the rule.
#
# CF_API_TOKEN  : API token (NOT the Global API Key, NOT the token ID).
#                 Cloudflare tokens are 40 chars, [A-Za-z0-9_-]. If yours is a different
#                 length you copied the wrong field — the token value is shown exactly once,
#                 on the page right after you create it.
#
#                 Create at: dash.cloudflare.com > My Profile > API Tokens > Create Custom Token
#                 Permission rows (note the SCOPE selector on the left of each row — it
#                 defaults to Account, and the zone permissions are a different list):
#
#                   Zone     > App Security          > Zone WAF            > Edit
#                   Account  > Rules & Configuration > Account Rule Lists  > Edit   (only if
#                                                                                    managing IP lists)
#
#                 Zone Resources: Include > Specific zone > <your domain>
#                 Set a TTL. Optionally restrict to your client IP.
#
#                 Near-misses that authenticate and then 403: "Account WAF" (account-level
#                 config, not your zone's custom rules) and "Account Rulesets". The endpoint
#                 path tells you the scope: /zones/... needs a Zone permission.
#
# CF_ZONE_ID    : Overview tab of the domain in the dashboard, right column.
# CF_ACCOUNT_ID : only needed for the `lists` command. Same place, under Zone ID.

CF_API_TOKEN=
CF_ZONE_ID=
# CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
